","content":"Mysterious Places and Urban Legends are good for local supernatural phenomena for Beasts to investigate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T10:19:11-07:00","id":1136623,"title":"","content":"It's not from another line, but what might be an interesting read are the Seven Thesis of Monster Theory by Jeffrey Jerome Cohen. Might not be an easy read, but certainly helpful in regards to doing things with monsters in general, and Beasts specifically, imo.<br /> On Monsters by Stephen T. Asma was recommended and cited as an inspiration in a thread, I think?<br /> <br /> I also think Hunter books are helpful, because they look at monsters from a different perspective and help you see what is scary about them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}